CLR (rocclr) H2D copy routing (gfx803): never read a pinned or
registered/locked host source directly from the copy engine, and never pin
a pageable host source. Force the GPU-staging path (CPU memcpy into a GPU
staging buffer, then a copy from that buffer) for every H2D copy. This is
the Host-to-Device mirror of d2h-staged-copy.patch.

WHY
---
For an H2D copy above GPU_PINNED_MIN_XFER_SIZE from pageable memory,
DmaBlitManager::writeBuffer pins the source. That creates a userptr buffer
object (BO). When the process later frees or unmaps that memory, the KFD
MMU notifier evicts every user queue of the process and restores them
afterwards. On gfx803 this shows up in two ways:

- The process hangs on its next GPU command with no amdgpu or kfd error in
  dmesg. /sys/class/kfd/kfd/proc/<pid>/stats_*/evicted_ms, sampled every
  2 ms, shows dozens of evict/restore cycles during model.cuda(). A hung run
  has one more eviction while the queue is idle, and the next packet then
  never completes. Ringing the doorbell again from gdb does not help, so the
  queue itself is lost. Kernel 7.0.0 hangs 10 of 12 short torch runs. The
  root cause is not identified. The evidence points at the kernel's
  evict/restore of an idle gfx8 queue or at the gfx8 CP firmware, and this
  repo builds neither. Later kernels have not shown the hang.
- A torch weight-processing loop that moves large layers to the GPU one at
  a time (33.5 MB and up) and runs `.abs().max()` on each faults with
  "Memory access fault ... Page not present" (read from TC0).

Setting GPU_PINNED_MIN_XFER_SIZE=4096 (staging for every copy) keeps
evicted_ms at 0, and the hang rate drops from 10/12 to 0/12. See
https://github.com/Schaka/rocm-gfx803/issues/9 for the measurements.

A pinned or registered source (hipHostMalloc, hipHostRegister) is not
pinned by CLR, so it does not create a userptr BO here. It goes through
the staging path as well, so that H2D takes the same route as D2H and no
engine access to host memory can be outstanding when the host buffer goes
away. d2h-staged-copy.patch measured that fault for engine writes to host
memory. Nobody has measured it for engine reads.

WHAT
---
- DmaBlitManager::writeBuffer: pass enablePin=false, so a pageable source
  is copied into a GPU staging buffer by the CPU and never pinned.
  KernelBlitManager::writeBuffer already passes kEnablePin=false for its
  shader-copy path and calls DmaBlitManager::writeBuffer for its SDMA path,
  so it needs no change.
- submitWriteMemory (WriteMemoryCommand): when the source resolves to a
  registered/locked host buffer (hostMemory != nullptr), route through the
  staged writeBuffer instead of the accelerated copyBuffer. The staged path
  reads the source on the CPU when the copy is submitted, and for an H2D
  copy it does not wait for earlier queue work. A releaseGpuMemoryFence()
  wait first keeps queue order: an earlier GPU write to the pinned buffer
  has landed before the CPU reads it, as it had with copyBuffer.
- copyMemory (CopyMemoryCommand): when the source is
  host-memory-direct-access, route through the staged writeBuffer instead
  of copyBuffer. writeBuffer applies its origin to the destination only, so
  the source address is the source VA plus `realSrcOrigin[0]`. Without that
  offset, a copy from the middle of a pinned buffer uploads from its start.
  The same queue-order wait comes first.

COST
---
Each H2D copy gets an extra CPU memcpy, done by the submitting thread. A
copy from a pinned source also waits on the CPU for earlier work in its
queue, so a `non_blocking=True` upload from pinned memory no longer
overlaps with the kernels queued before it. Nobody has measured the
throughput cost yet.

Applies AFTER d2h-null-dsthost.patch (the copyMemory hunk extends the
branch that patch rewrites).

NOT YET RE-VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE. This file is generated against the
pinned 10.2 tree. An earlier version of the same change ran on the ROCm
10.1 stack, on an RX 580 2048SP with torch 2.14. Without it, the layer loop above hung or
faulted on layer 13 (net.blocks.0.mlp.layer2.weight, [2048, 8192]). With
it, all 515 layers ran clean, round trips of float32, float16, bfloat16,
int32 and int8 up to 134 MB were correct, and 50 alloc/copy/free cycles of
33.5 MB unpinned tensors passed.
